! `alas-epsilon-us.in` with the fully-relativistic datasets and spin-orbit
! coupling on: the first dielectric response of an **augmented spinor** (P98).
!
! The cell, the cutoffs, the grid and the wedge are `alas-epsilon-us.in`'s and
! only the two pseudopotential files change, so what separates this case from
! that one is the spin structure alone. That is what makes it the discriminating
! check: a scalar-relativistic dataset run with `noncolin = .true.` has
! `fcoef = 1`, so `qq_so` is block diagonal, `dpqq_so` is the scalar dipole on
! both spin blocks and the recombination inside `int3` collapses -- every term
! this phase wrote is exercised and none of them is *distinguished*. A
! fully-relativistic file couples the two components through `fcoef` and none of
! the three collapses.
!
! AlAs is chosen over a heavier crystal because the answer stays close to the
! scalar one, so the comparison against `ph.x` is not dominated by the coupling
! itself: what is being validated is the augmentation terms in the spinor
! response, not the size of spin-orbit splitting in arsenic.
!
! The reference is regenerated with the vendored `ph.x`
! (`alas-epsilon-us-soc.ph.in`) and committed as
! `reference.out.ph-alas-epsilon-us-soc`.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.575, nat = 2, ntyp = 2,
    ecutwfc = 25.0, ecutrho = 200.0,
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Al  26.98  Al.rel-pbe-n-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF
 As  74.92  As.rel-pbe-n-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Al 0.00 0.00 0.00
 As 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 1 1 1
